# Expectation record for 05_pointers/001_arithmetic_on_arrays.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 001_arithmetic_on_arrays
area               = 05_pointers
description        = Pointer increment, decrement, indexing and element scaling within one array
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Every pointer stays within its own array, and no pointer is advanced past one element
beyond the last.  Element offsets are expressed as differences between pointers into the
same array object, never by subtracting character views of distinct element subobjects,
which would not be a subtraction within one object.  No address is printed and no pointer
one past the end is dereferenced.  Every property is computed twice, once from literal constants and once from
volatile-qualified storage, so the constant folder's answer cannot stand in for the
one the backend computes at run time.
No header is included and printf is declared by hand, so nothing here depends on a
standard-library header bcc does not ship.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
